            ABSTRACT                                             We present V2N (Video to Notes), 1 the first complete\n                                                                                                                     VPT system: four task-specific heads (onset, offset, key\n                                        Audio-based piano transcription performs well on onset,                      hold, and velocity; Figure 1) are trained jointly over 1 s of\n                                        pitch, and velocity, but the sustain pedal lets sound per-                   video and supervised at every frame rather than only at the\n                                        sist long after key release, so audio systems predict pedal-                 window center. Our contributions are:\n                                        extended offsets rather than physical key release. Yet ex-\n                                        isting Visual Piano Transcription (VPT) systems focus on                      • Video-only complete MIDI transcription. V2N\n                                        onset detection from short video windows, offset accuracy                       matches Li et al. [9] on PianoVAM Onset and surpasses\n                                        lags onset by a wide margin, and note-level velocity has                        all prior VPT on R3 Onset, substantially improves phys-\n                                        not been reported. To address these gaps, we present V2N                        ical key-release prediction, and is the first video-only\narXiv:2608.03419v1 [cs.SD] 4 Aug 2026\n\n\n\n\n                                        (Video to Notes), the first complete VPT system: a shared                       system to report note-level velocity F1, achieving state-\n                                        temporal backbone feeds task-specific heads for onset, off-                     of-the-art results on PianoVAM and R3.\n          